WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.219 --> 00:00:13.380
there, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.222
I'm reading a really suspenseful fantasy book, Dragons of Gold.

3
00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:23.224
It's about some magical dragons that protect the kingdom of the Golden Emperor.

4
00:00:24.745 --> 00:00:26.205
Oh, that reminds me!

5
00:00:26.946 --> 00:00:29.387
I want to tell you about our latest landmarks lesson.

6
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:31.348
Yesterday in Mr.

7
00:00:31.388 --> 00:00:35.250
Fay's class, we learned about an awesome place in Beijing, China.

8
00:00:36.391 --> 00:00:42.976
The Forbidden City, which was the home of Chinese emperors for nearly five hundred years, is the largest

9
00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:44.958
palace complex in the world.

10
00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:52.703
And get this: the complex is decorated with thousands of cool-looking dragons, too many of them to even

11
00:00:52.783 --> 00:01:01.171
count! Construction of the Forbidden City began in 1406, when the third emperor from the Ming dynasty decided

12
00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:04.312
to build a new palace complex in the city of Beijing.

13
00:01:06.073 --> 00:01:12.815
According to most historical accounts, it took thousands of people working for about 14 years to build the

14
00:01:12.876 --> 00:01:13.676
Forbidden City.

15
00:01:14.736 --> 00:01:19.238
But what's really amazing is how the workers moved some of the building materials.

16
00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:27.806
To transport heavy blocks of stone thousands of miles, workers created ice paths by pouring water on the

17
00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:28.747
frozen ground.

18
00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:33.529
The slippery ice made it much easier to drag the stone blocks along.

19
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:40.013
And then, when huge logs needed to be moved for some of the buildings, guess what the workers

20
00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:45.095
did They waited for heavy rains to come and wash the logs into rivers!

21
00:01:46.476 --> 00:01:51.959
From there, boatmen could steer the logs into the Grand Canal, and then all the wood could be

22
00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:53.140
towed to Beijing.

23
00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:56.585
Now that's what I call a smart idea!

24
00:01:58.425 --> 00:02:01.866
The Forbidden City was finally completed around 1420.

25
00:02:03.047 --> 00:02:11.389
When the emperor moved into the palace complex, it had over 800 buildings with 9,999 rooms!

26
00:02:12.369 --> 00:02:21.692
Wow! Chinese emperors continued living in the Forbidden City until 1912, when the Chinese Revolution ended imperial rule.

27
00:02:22.784 --> 00:02:27.107
The last emperor, Puyi, was only about six years old at the time.

28
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:34.152
And for the next 12 years, he and his family were basically imprisoned inside the Forbidden City.

29
00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:40.236
By now you're probably wondering why the palace complex is called the Forbidden City.

30
00:02:41.437 --> 00:02:42.498
Well, Mr.

31
00:02:42.558 --> 00:02:46.640
Fay explained that it was because most people were forbidden from entering it.

32
00:02:47.781 --> 00:02:51.504
Even members of the imperial family were restricted from some areas.

33
00:02:52.652 --> 00:02:58.674
There was only one person who could go wherever he wanted in the complex—the emperor himself!

34
00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:06.716
Still today the first thing you notice as you approach the Forbidden City are the enormous red walls—almost

35
00:03:06.796 --> 00:03:09.097
ten meters high—surrounding the place.

36
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:12.718
The Meridian Gate serves as the main entrance.

37
00:03:13.978 --> 00:03:20.020
This gate has five different doors, but back in imperial times, people couldn't just walk through any door

38
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:20.520
they wanted.

39
00:03:21.808 --> 00:03:24.169
Instead they had to enter based on their rank.

40
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:30.853
The emperor's family members used the door at the west side of the gate while low-ranking ministers went

41
00:03:30.873 --> 00:03:32.354
through the door on the east side.

42
00:03:33.575 --> 00:03:36.076
Only the emperor entered through the middle door.

43
00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:43.100
Inside the Forbidden City there are two main areas: the Outer Court and the Inner Court.

44
00:03:44.301 --> 00:03:49.644
During imperial rule, business was conducted in the Outer Court, which has three main halls.

45
00:03:51.476 --> 00:03:58.081
The Hall of Supreme Harmony is the largest wooden building in China and with its elaborate decorations, it's

46
00:03:58.181 --> 00:04:01.924
probably the most magnificent structure inside the whole complex.

47
00:04:03.025 --> 00:04:08.529
In front of the building, there's a huge open space, big enough to fit everyone who lived in

48
00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:12.172
the Forbidden City—about 100,000 people!

49
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:19.426
The Hall of Supreme Harmony was also where the emperors' throne sat and where they held ceremonies for

50
00:04:19.486 --> 00:04:23.891
things like weddings, the New Year, and sending soldiers out to war.

51
00:04:24.952 --> 00:04:28.536
Emperors lived with their families and servants inside the Inner Court.

52
00:04:29.036 --> 00:04:32.500
And by "servants," I don't mean just a housekeeper or two.

53
00:04:33.521 --> 00:04:36.744
The Chinese emperors had thousands of servants.

54
00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:39.768
No wonder the Forbidden City is so huge!

55
00:04:41.268 --> 00:04:47.732
Just beyond the Inner Court is the Imperial Garden with fishponds, sculptures, and a small temple.

56
00:04:48.792 --> 00:04:52.874
Mr. Fay said the garden was a place where emperors could relax and meditate.

57
00:04:53.935 --> 00:04:59.118
I wonder if they spent a lot of time out there—if you ask me, ruling a whole empire

58
00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.779
sounds pretty stressful!

59
00:05:01.799 --> 00:05:07.062
While the buildings in the Forbidden City are all scarlet in color, the roofs are lined with yellow

60
00:05:07.122 --> 00:05:10.484
tiles since yellow symbolized the emperor's power.

61
00:05:11.528 --> 00:05:17.070
Many of the decorations inside the palaces are yellow or gold too, including the bricks used for the

62
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.932
floors. Remember what I said earlier about all the dragons inside the Forbidden City?

63
00:05:24.192 --> 00:05:32.835
Well, we found out that dragons symbolize important imperial characteristics such as benevolence, excellence, and heroism.

64
00:05:34.195 --> 00:05:36.616
That's why the Forbidden City has so many of them.

65
00:05:37.848 --> 00:05:45.453
There's an incredible "dragon pavement" with dragons carved out of stone; dragon statues perched on top of rooftops;

66
00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:51.176
and the Nine Dragon Screen, a really famous glazed tile screen.

67
00:05:52.397 --> 00:05:57.480
If you ever get to see the screen, you might notice that one tile looks slightly different from

68
00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:57.960
the rest.

69
00:05:59.101 --> 00:06:04.084
That's because right before an emperor was going to inspect the screen, a tile broke.

70
00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.723
So the craftsmen, who didn't want to get in trouble, quickly made a wooden tile and stuck it

71
00:06:10.783 --> 00:06:11.304
in place.

72
00:06:12.384 --> 00:06:14.465
Luckily the emperor never noticed!

73
00:06:16.326 --> 00:06:19.448
Each year millions of tourists visit the Forbidden City.

74
00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:28.634
Many head to the Palace Museum to see artifacts from imperial China such as precious silks, jewelry, porcelain,

75
00:06:29.034 --> 00:06:32.656
jade, bronzes, and other valuable works of art.

76
00:06:33.844 --> 00:06:38.689
Today a lot of the buildings inside the Forbidden City are set up just as they were hundreds

77
00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:39.429
of years ago.

78
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:41.491
According to Mr.

79
00:06:41.531 --> 00:06:47.697
Fay, that makes it easy to imagine the royal court back in the days when servants, couriers, and

80
00:06:47.757 --> 00:06:50.699
important government officials were bustling around.

81
00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:55.284
Well, I hope you enjoyed your quick tour of the Forbidden City.

82
00:06:56.545 --> 00:06:59.828
If you'll excuse me now, I'm dying to get back to my book.

83
00:07:01.036 --> 00:07:06.821
The dragons are about to rescue the emperor's son, the Golden Prince, from the evil Jade Warlock!
